hubbs is the second of two make up ascensions I needed to do to repay my 
RGRN karma after posting savescummed ascensions 15 or so years ago.  Needless
to say I was thoroughly yelled at, so I went back and starting playing the
game the right way.  4 years ago I completed my first legitimate ascension,
and this week I finally completed the other.  For some reason both of these
ascensions have occurred in the devnull tournament...I guess schererazade is
my lucky server :>

My nethack characters generally are homages to characters in film.  hubbs is
patterned after Michael Hubbs from the James Melkonian film "The Stoned Age",
who was the first character I thought of when I thought of an orcish
barbarian.  In the scummed ascension post, he never wore dragon scale,
instead preferring a leather jacket, but when you scum your ascension you can
pretty much do what you want.  In reality, I was tired of playing a Barbarian
(and my wife was sick of watching "The Stoned Age" before tournament season
every year...admittedly it's much funnier if you are just some dude in your
20s) so I ended up dropping the affectations and just got down to business.

hubbs had some luck with the way the dungeon was layed out.  There was an
altar at the mines junction on the second level, and four more down through
the 21st level, two of them attended.  An early blessed smoky potion (full
healing in this game, but only the first ever had a djinni) outfitted hubbs
with the blessed Master Key of Thievery, which really made the difference for
the rest of the game.  This wish came early on, before even reaching Sokoban,
which rewarded him with an Amulet of Reflection.

The Master Key made the gnomish mines much easier than normal, but
remembering an early failed ascension run where the wizard stole the amulet
back and then just disappeared, nowhere to be found, hubbs spent some time
early in the game digging out the mines, just in case he had to pursue the
wizard to the bottom ofthe mines and escape back to the top.  Taking this
extra time in the mines paid off, as by the end of the sojourn through the
mines he had collected two blessed magic lamps and another blessed potion of
full healing.  

Hubbs avoided sacrificing at altars in hopes of wishing for the blessed
Longbow of Diana, but while exploring the lower levels of the dungeons of
doom, he stepped on a polymorph trap and was transformed into a master mind
flayer.  This was rediculously cool, as he didn't lose any armor and was able
to suck most monsters dry in one hit.  So though he lacked a wand of death he
ran up to the quest level to suck as many ogres and trolls as possible. 
Unfortunately he was returned to orcish form shortly after starting the
quest, but since it was getting later in the month and I was starting to get
pressed for time, I went for it.  This was problematic, as no tinning kit
ever made an appearance during this game.  But using a tortuously long
strategy of "bring the next troll upstairs, kill him, and dig out the level
until you get hungry", he eventually ate his way through to Thoth Amon.  No
wands of death at this point in the game, but a wand of sleep did the trick.

Since the idea of wishing for another artifact was now moot, hubbs went ahead
and started converting altars and getting set for the initial run of potion
and scroll identification and production of Holy Water.  Cleaver made things
go much quicker, especially as a couple of blessed scrolls of enchant weapon
bumped it to +3.  

The amulet from Sokoban and the discovery of a ring of levitation made short
work of Medusa.  But before taking the castle, I had to figure out what to do 
with the wishes.  I couldn't go for another artifact since I'd picked up the 
Heart of Ahriman.  Armor would have been a logical choice, but two years
before hubbs had been at this point, planning for an assault on the castle
and using up wishes on armor.  Last time an attempt to take out the Liches
guarding the castle failed, and I wanted this to be hubbs' last game.  Though
I had a potential 3 wishes, only one of the lamps came through.  2 blessed
scolls of genocide later and I was done with L's and h's.  Now that hubbs had
genocide identified, he took the additional step of writing another scroll
and getting rid of g's (there were gremlins on medusa's level, and I remember
exactly how irritating that was during the scummed assension 15 years
earlier).

After that the castle was pretty straightforward.  hubbs pulled Silver Dragon
Scale Mail, a Cloak of Magic Resistance, and Gauntlets of Power out of the
castle's wand, as somewhere in the melee he had picked up some speed boots.
More work at the altars yielded Stormbringer, and two-weaponing it with a
silver sabre made Gehennom equally straightforward.  hubbs was on a good
enough roll he cleared it out to the bottom and cleaned out the wizards tower
to grab all of the interesting items before starting the run.

After the final bout of identification, intermittently useful potion mixing,
and scroll writing, Stormy was +5, the sabre was +4, AC was -34 (hubbs had a
couple more wishes available at this point but I wasn't sure a +2 t-shirt was
really going to be worth it, as he might need them for something else during
the run), and he had over 400 hit points.  The wand of polymorph had JUST
been identified, and the new moon had just begun.  I still had about a week
left before the end of the tournament, but I wasn't sure how much game time
was going to be available.  Looking things over, I found that with the wishes
hubbs had used, his ascension kit was actually already complete, so he'd
really only be polypiling for spares.  I wasn't sure if I was ready to try
for a conduct, but everything was really in place for it.  Hubbs also hadn't
yet discovered Fort Ludios, but as I thought about it I realized exactly how
done I was with playing him.  The Valkyrie ascension was more fun, as I can't
get enough of throwing Mjollnir :>

Finally I said "screw it" and made for the wizard.  2 wands of death in
hubbs' stash at this point got him the book pretty fast, and he had
previously marked out the vibrating square.  Things started to get a
little hairy in the Sanctum, so hubbs put on the ring of conflict he'd
identified earlier and focused on zapping Rodney whenever he would reappear.
There was a scary moment after opening the secret door to the High Priest's
chamber when Rodney appeared again and dished out Double Trouble, but as luck
would have it they lined up all in a row, and one zap from the wand of death
took out all three of them.

The run upstairs went fairly easily...the mysterious force only struck twice,
and hubbs had spent a good amount of time digging out passages between the 
staircases all the way up, which paid off.  Most of Rodney's reappearences
took place in the sanctum, and hubbs still had plenty of charges in the wand
of death, so he didn't pose much of a problem on the way up.  The elemental
planes went by quickly except for fire, of course, and eventually hubbs had
to turn off the conflict cause it was making things take too long.  

At the Astral plane hubbs took a little too long killing adventurers in the
lobby before putting on the towel and taking stock.  Death was already
through the door so hubbs started running for Famine, got caught by Death in
the doorway, reverted to hand to hand, and realized that Death is pretty
vulnerable to the Stormy/sabre combo (hubbs had plenty of hit points, and
Death only ever got a couple of shots in before expiring).  Still, since
hubbs was already committing to a direction, he took out famine with the
wand of death, got to what turned out to be the wrong altar, and turned
around to head back toward the direction Death had initially come from.  By
this time hubbs had done something or other to piss of the Angel of Set, so
he opted for conflict and a stack of wands of teleport to make the whole
process a little more streamlined.  It figures of course that Set's altar was
the one Death had been initially guarding.  There was an adventurer there as
well waving Magicbane around, but by the time I noticed, hubbs was already
standing on the altar...

